<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grand Village of the Natchez</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fatherland Site</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grand Village of the Natchez is an archaeological site on St Catherine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> in Natchez, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, the main ceremonial centre of the Natchez people in the late 17th and early 18th centuries. It holds platform mounds on which stood the house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-sun">Great Sun</a>, the sacred ruler, and a temple with a perpetual fire. French writers described it before the Natchez were destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1729–1731.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grand-village-of-the-natchez.jpg" alt="Grand Village of the Natchez" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herb Roe, www.chromesun.com. The original uploader was Heironymous Rowe at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chromesun_grandvillage_of_the_natchez01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
